    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d433">

                <pb facs="0406" n="396" sameAs="frus:frus1941v05/pg396"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.92/34</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Assistant Secretary of State</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Berle</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-12-30">December 30, 1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I gather that the British Government agrees entirely with the line taken by the
                    Thai Minister; he is particularly anxious to let drive at the Prime Minister of
                    Thailand, who seems to have sold out the country.</p>
                <p>Under the circumstances, unless FE<anchor n="91" xml:id="d433-f1"/> has some
                    reason to the contrary, I propose that we continue to recognize the Thai
                    Minister; that we let him go ahead and develop such Free Thai movement as he
                    can; and that we use the Department’s power to certify his right to dispose of a
                    limited amount of money. I should be glad to have a recommendation as to the
                    amount of money we ought to advise the Thai Minister that he might need.</p>
                <p>Unless FE disagrees, I should be glad if FD<anchor n="92" xml:id="d433-f2"/>
                    would go ahead and get the material together for the appropriate certifications.
                    The assistance of Treasury will be needed in locating the assets.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>A. A. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berle, Jr.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
